# Infrastructure as Code — AWS ECS Fargate (Terraform)

This directory deploys **{{projectName}}** to AWS: the Airflow api-server
behind an internet-facing **ALB**, a core service (scheduler + dag-processor
+ triggerer) on **ECS Fargate**, **RDS Postgres** as the metadata DB, task
logs on **S3**, secrets in **Secrets Manager**, a **CloudWatch dashboard**
(+ optional email alarms), and **hybrid executors**: ordinary tasks run via
LocalExecutor inside the scheduler container (no Fargate cold start); tasks
that opt in with `executor=os.getenv("DEDICATED_TASK_EXECUTOR")` get their
own Fargate container via the AWS ECS executor.

Everything is driven through `dude iac <command>` — see `dude help iac`. All
commands are environment-scoped via `--env <name>` (required, no default); run
any of them without it to list the environments found under
`terraform/environments/`.

> **Lock the door.** The UI has authentication ({{#if withEntraId}}Entra ID{{else}}native login{{/if}}),
> but still restrict `allowed_cidrs` in `terraform.tfvars` to your org's
> networks before running anything real.

## Layout

```
iac/
├── runner/Dockerfile          # container the IaC toolchain runs in (yours to edit)
└── terraform/
    ├── main.tf                # VPC, security groups, Cloud Map, ALB, TLS+DNS
    ├── rds.tf                 # metadata DB (RDS Postgres)
    ├── secrets.tf             # core + app secrets, S3 task-logs bucket
    ├── ecs.tf                 # cluster, web/core services, worker & migrate task defs
    ├── iam.tf                 # execution role + task role (S3 logs, ECS executor)
    ├── variables.tf           # all knobs, documented
    ├── outputs.tf             # airflow_url, cluster/services, secret ARN, …
    ├── bootstrap/             # ONCE per project: S3 state + DynamoDB lock + ECR
    └── environments/
        └── dev/
            ├── backend.hcl        # where this env's remote state lives
            └── terraform.tfvars   # this env's knobs (sizing, domain, image tag)
```

## First deploy

```bash
dude iac login --env dev                                  # AWS SSO / profile
dude iac bootstrap --state-bucket-prefix <org> --env dev --yes
dude iac init --env dev
dude iac apply --env dev                                  # ~10 min (RDS)
{{#if withEntraId}}
dude iac secrets --env dev --set AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=<from the app registration>
# and add https://<airflow_url>/auth/oauth-authorized/azure to the Entra app's
# redirect URIs (get the URL with: dude iac output --env dev)
{{/if}}
dude iac migrate --env dev                                # airflow db migrate{{#unless withEntraId}} + admin user{{/unless}}
dude iac ship --env dev                                   # build + push + deploy
dude iac status --env dev
```
{{#unless withEntraId}}

The admin password is generated by Terraform — read it with (use the same
`--profile` as `dude iac`: whatever you passed with `--profile`, otherwise
`$AWS_PROFILE`, otherwise the `{{projectName}}-dev` convention):

```bash
aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --profile {{projectName}}-dev \
  --secret-id {{projectName}}/dev/core --query SecretString --output text | jq -r .admin_password
```
{{/unless}}

## Day-2

| Task | Command |
| ---- | ------- |
| Ship a code change (DAGs are baked into the image) | `dude iac ship --env dev` |
| Add/read app secrets | `dude iac secrets --env dev [--set K=V] [--roll]` |
| Plain env vars | `app_environment` in terraform.tfvars + `apply` |
| Airflow version bump | edit both Dockerfiles → `ship` → `dude iac migrate` |
| Health overview (services, heartbeats, worker exits) | `dude iac status --env dev` |
| Live logs (one component: `--service scheduler`, `worker`, …) | `dude iac logs --env dev --follow` |
| Metrics dashboard (ECS/ALB/RDS + recent errors) | `dude iac output --env dev` → `dashboard_url` |
| Email alarms (UI down, core CPU, DB storage) | set `alarm_email` in terraform.tfvars + `apply` |
| New environment | `dude iac new-env --env staging` |
| Tear down | `dude iac destroy --env dev` |

See `docs/docs/deploy.md` (or `dude docs`) for the full walkthrough, including
how the hybrid executors and the example DAGs use this infrastructure.
